# tests/recursion/inlookaround.rxt -- module `recursion` ([DD-14]): SS3.4(d)/(e) (a call INSIDE a lookaround) and SS3.5's mirror image (a call TO a group whose lexical home is one)
#
# GENERATED BY tests/recursion/gen_corpus.py, and that is a property rather
# than a convenience: every expectation below -- including every `g` line --
# was produced by driving the cell through libpcre2 10.46 (via the committed
# ctypes binding at docs/design/subroutines_measurements/probes/sr_oracle.py,
# which borrows la_oracle.py -> br_oracle.py -> pcre2_ctypes.py) BEFORE it was
# written. The generator never asks pcrec anything.
#
# THE SOLE ORACLE IS LIBPCRE2 -- THERE IS NO PYTHON ARM, AND THAT IS AN
# ABSENCE RATHER THAN A DIVERGENCE (design SS10.1). python3 `re` has no
# subroutine-call construct at all: every one of the nine call spellings plus
# both zero spellings raises `re.error` at compile time. `\\1` and `(?P=n)`
# DO compile in python, but they are `backrefs`' reference construct, not this
# module's call construct (design SS2.1's one-cell discriminator is why
# `spellings.rxt` opens with it) -- so marking this corpus python-verifiable
# on the strength of those two spellings would be checking the wrong module.
#
# `(?(DEFINE)...)` DOES NOT APPEAR ANYWHERE IN THIS CORPUS. It is module
# `conditionals`'s construct until D71 decision 4's registry row lands
# ([DD-14] wave F, not started when this corpus was written); every callee-
# only body below uses the oracle-verified `{0}`-callee idiom instead
# (design SS2.5/SS4.4c).
#
# NOTHING IN src/ IMPLEMENTS A SUBROUTINE CALL YET. Every ordinary `m`/`n`
# block in this corpus is therefore EXPECTED to report a pattern-compile
# failure under today's harness (`requires module 'recursion'`) -- that is
# the correct, documented state before [DD-14] wave B+C lands (docs/
# testing.md's "expected-unsupported" policy), not a corpus defect.
#
# Design: docs/design/subroutines_design.md. Decisions: docs/dev/decisions.md
# D71 (the give-up-code axis, D71.1) and D72 (PCREC_ERR_INTERNAL).

# ====================================================================
# A CALL INSIDE A LOOKBEHIND NEEDS A WIDTH (design SS3.4d, and its 2026-08-24 [DD-14.LB] amendment) -- the width analysis descends into `A_CALL` through the call graph's `maxw` memo, and it does so in `pcrec_postresolve` because the parse hook runs before any callee is bound. Fixed-PER-BRANCH is still the shipped subset: what a call changes is WHEN the width is known, never WHICH widths qualify.
# ====================================================================

# THE CELL THE DEFERRED RE-CHECK EXISTS FOR (wave B+C's parked cell 1): a callee of fixed width 2, refused as "unbounded" until the width question was moved past the call graph. The two `n` rows are the control that the lookbehind is doing work rather than being ignored.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>ab)){0}ab(?<=(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "ab" 0 2
n "a"
n "abab"

# THE CALL IS NOT ALONE IN ITS BRANCH -- width 1 + 2. A branch is a CONCATENATION and `pcrec_maxw` sums it; a re-check that only understood a bare call would pass the cell above and fail this one.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>ab)){0}zab(?<=z(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "zab" 0 3
n "ab"

# A TWO-HOP ACYCLIC CHAIN: the lookbehind calls `g`, which calls `h`. Width 3 is only reachable by iterating the `maxw` fixpoint twice -- a single pass over the graph settles `h` and leaves `g` at UNBOUNDED, which would refuse this cell.
pattern ^(?:(?<h>cd)){0}(?:(?<g>(?&h)e)){0}cde(?<=(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "cde" 0 3
n "cd"

# FIXED-PER-BRANCH, THROUGH CALLS, WITH THE WIDTHS DIFFERING: two top-level branches of widths 1 and 2, each a call. This is the form pcrec's subset ships (`(?<=a|bc)x`'s shape), and it is the cell wave B+C's parked note MEANT to be describing -- unlike cell 2 below, the alternation is at the lookbehind body's OWN top level.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>a)){0}(?:(?<h>bc)){0}xbc(?<=(?&g)|(?&h))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "xbc" 0 3
n "xa"
n "xz"

# the same pattern shape entered through the OTHER branch, so both widths in the table are exercised rather than just the first one the emitter tries.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>a)){0}(?:(?<h>bc)){0}xa(?<=(?&g)|(?&h))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "xa" 0 2

# THE NEGATIVE POLARITY, and it is the one that matters most: on `(?<!` a width that is too SMALL is a FALSE MATCH rather than a lost one (lookaround_design.md SS3.4, sabotage row S-LA11), so a `maxw` memo that under-estimated a callee would show up here and nowhere else.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>ab)){0}ac(?<!(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "ac" 0 2
n "ab"

# MIXED BRANCHES: two call-FREE branches of width 1 and one CALL branch of width 2, in one body. The deferral is decided per-LOOKBEHIND (`pcrec_has_call(body)`), not per-branch, so this whole table is computed at the late timing -- including the two branches the parse hook could have answered.
pattern ^(?:(?<h>bc)){0}xbc(?<=a|b|(?&h))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "xbc" 0 3

# the same body entered through a CALL-FREE branch, which is the half a deferral that mislaid the non-call branches would lose.
pattern ^(?:(?<h>bc)){0}xa(?<=a|b|(?&h))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "xa" 0 2

# and its negative control -- no branch matches.
pattern ^(?:(?<h>bc)){0}xz(?<=a|b|(?&h))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
n "xz"

# SS2.4's BRANCH-ORDER CELL, REACHED THROUGH CALLS, and the `g` lines are the whole assertion: PCRE2 tries a lookbehind's top-level branches in WRITTEN order, so the width-1 branch wins and group 3 -- the capture WRAPPING the call, not one inside the callee -- reports (1,2). The deferred re-check fills `u.look.widths` from the END of the left-nested spine exactly as the parse hook does; get that backwards and this cell reports (0,2) on a still-correct span. NOTE WHY THE CAPTURE IS OUTSIDE THE CALLEE: a group written INSIDE one is restored by the RETURN (design SS3.1), so `(?<=(?&g)|(?&h))` with the captures in `g` and `h` reports NOTHING and cannot see the order at all -- measured on 10.46 before this cell was written.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>a)){0}(?:(?<h>aa)){0}aa(?<=((?&g))|((?&h)))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 -1 -1
g 3 1 2
g 4 -1 -1

# THE SAME TWO BRANCHES IN THE OTHER WRITTEN ORDER, which is what makes the pair a discriminator rather than a single observation: here the width-2 branch is first and group 3 reports (0,2). One cell alone is satisfied by 'longest first', by 'shortest first', and by written order.
pattern ^(?:(?<h>aa)){0}(?:(?<g>a)){0}aa(?<=((?&h))|((?&g)))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 -1 -1
g 3 0 2
g 4 -1 -1

# THE CALL IS INSIDE A NESTED LOOKAHEAD INSIDE THE LOOKBEHIND, so it contributes 0 to both widths and the body is fixed width 2. The parse hook DEFERS this one anyway (`pcrec_has_call` descends into A_LOOK) and the deferred ask returns the identical table -- the cell pins that the over-deferral is harmless, which is the claim src/parse/mod_lookaround.c makes at the deferral site.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>b)){0}ab(?<=a(?=(?&g))b)$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "ab" 0 2

# WAVE B+C's PARKED CELL 2, RULED. ONE top-level branch -- an `A_CALL` -- of width 1..2, because the alternation is inside the CALLEE. That is `(?<=(a|bc))x` reached through a call: lookaround_design.md SS2.5 CHARTERS the longest-first step-back loop it needs and does not ship it, and tests/lookaround/refused.rxt pins the call-free twin with this same citation. Note the DIAGNOSTIC below, which is how the two questions were told apart: before [DD-14.LB] pcrec said "unbounded" here, a claim about the call graph that was FALSE; it now says "1..2", which is a claim about the shipped subset and is true. Letting a body that is exactly one `A_CALL` borrow the callee's top-level branch split was considered and rejected -- it does not generalise (`(?<=x(?&g))` is one branch again) and it would invert SS2.4's MEASURED ordering, since PCRE2 tries a single variable branch step-back LONGEST-FIRST while branches are tried in written order.
# MEASURED: libpcre2 10.46 ACCEPTS this pattern and answers (0,2) on 'ab' -- pcrec's refusal is a CAPABILITY limit, stated as one (D26 tier 2: which constructs are real is exact, what pcrec builds is a separate statement)
# MEASURED, build/pcrec under --features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups: pcrec: variable-length lookbehind is not implemented: every alternative of a lookbehind must have a fixed length (this one can match 1..2 characters) (pattern offset 20)
pattern ^(?:(?<g>a|ab)){0}ab(?<=(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
perr

# a RECURSIVE callee inside a lookbehind has no bounded width -- libpcre2 itself refuses this (err 125), and pcrec now refuses it from `pcrec_postresolve` AT THE SAME OFFSET (26) with "this one is unbounded". The `maxw` fixpoint reaches UNBOUNDED here as a FIXED POINT: the published value saturates `mrl_sat_add` and never leaves the top (design SS3.4d: infinity for any callee in a cycle).
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 26: length of lookbehind assertion is not limited
pattern ^(?:(?<g>a(?&g)?b)){0}aabb(?<=(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
perr

# MUTUAL recursion, a cycle of length TWO. Refused by both, at the same offset (44). A `maxw` implementation that tested only SELF-recursion -- `target i calls target i` -- would compile this one and emit a back-step for an unbounded body.
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 44: length of lookbehind assertion is not limited
pattern ^(?:(?<g>a(?&h)?b)){0}(?:(?<h>(?&g))){0}aabb(?<=(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
perr

# THE CALLEE IS NOT IN A CYCLE AND STILL HAS NO WIDTH: `g` is acyclic and calls `h`, which is recursive. Refused by both, at the same offset (44). This is the cell that rules out `pcrec_callgraph_reaches(i, i)` as the maxw rule -- `g` is not in a cycle, so a cycle TEST answers "finite" here, while the fixpoint's SATURATION propagates the callee's UNBOUNDED into `g` for free. One mechanism instead of two.
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 44: length of lookbehind assertion is not limited
pattern ^(?:(?<h>a(?&h)?b)){0}(?:(?<g>(?&h)x)){0}abx(?<=(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
perr

# ====================================================================
# A CALL INSIDE A LOOKAHEAD OR AN ATOMIC GROUP IS ORDINARY (design SS3.4e) -- nothing in this module is special-cased for them.
# ====================================================================

# positive lookahead.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>ab)){0}(?=(?&g))ab$
features recursion,lookaround,named-groups
m "ab" 0 2

# negative lookahead.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>ab)){0}(?!(?&g))ac$
features recursion,lookaround,named-groups
m "ac" 0 2

# atomic group.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>ab)){0}(?>(?&g))$
features recursion,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "ab" 0 2

# ====================================================================
# SS3.5's MIRROR IMAGE: a call TO A GROUP WHOSE LEXICAL HOME lives inside a lookaround or an atomic group -- the sharpest finding of the design. The callee runs as its OWN region, forward, consuming, cut-free -- whatever its lexical wrapper does -- and each wrapper shape needs its own control that isolates the wrapper.
# ====================================================================

# W1: the callee leaves through ITS OWN exit, not the lookbehind's end-check-cut-and-restore. "ab" alone is the CONTROL -- the call must consume.
pattern ^ab(?<=(ab))(?1)$
features recursion,lookaround
m "abab" 0 4
g 1 0 2
n "ab"

# W2: the call RETRIES into "zy" -- a region whose lexical home is CUT on the assertion's own success. "xzc" is the control (the first alternative suffices).
pattern ^(?!(z|zy))x(?1)c$
features recursion,lookaround
m "xzyc" 0 4
g 1 -1 -1
m "xzc" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1

# W3: the call GIVES BACK "a" and takes "ab", though its lexical home is ATOMIC.
pattern ^(?>(a|ab))z(?1)c$
features recursion,atomic-groups
m "azabc" 0 5
g 1 0 1

# W3's CONTROL: the same language written INLINE, atomic wrapper kept, no call at all -- same answer, so the call did not change what the atomic wrapper permits, only WHO gets to retry into it.
pattern ^(?>(a|ab))z(?:a|ab)c$
features atomic-groups
m "azabc" 0 5
g 1 0 1

# W5: positive lookahead.
pattern ^(?=(a|ab))..(?1)$
features recursion,lookaround
m "abab" 0 4
g 1 0 1

# W5: a lookahead nested inside a quantified group.
pattern ^((?=(b))|a)+(?2)$
features recursion,lookaround
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 1 1
g 2 1 2
